WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.333
Welcome to Module 10 of the
Non-Person Identity learning path:

2
00:00:04.333 --> 00:00:06.600
find drift and file the evidence.

3
00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:11.837
This module is Gate 6, Lifecycle, with
Chapter 10 of the developer workbook.

4
00:00:11.837 --> 00:00:16.237
You'll run two days of drift against
a baseline a reviewer approved,

5
00:00:16.237 --> 00:00:19.277
compare two years of the
A I use-case inventory,

6
00:00:19.277 --> 00:00:22.717
package both days with a hash
for every file, chained,

7
00:00:22.717 --> 00:00:28.076
verify a package with a standard checksum
tool, and assemble a period for the ISSO.

8
00:00:28.076 --> 00:00:31.375
Then you'll break it on purpose,
and watch the checks refuse.

9
00:00:31.375 --> 00:00:35.612
If you're an ISSO reviewer,
this module is yours in full.

10
00:00:35.612 --> 00:00:40.550
The code runs on your laptop against
fixtures, in PowerShell and in Python.

11
00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:45.920
It's illustrative: tested against
fixtures, not run against a live tenant.

12
00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:51.708
Why does this module come at the end?

13
00:00:51.708 --> 00:00:55.065
Because every gate before
it ends in a state:

14
00:00:55.065 --> 00:00:57.957
an owner, a target, a credential, a scope.

15
00:00:57.957 --> 00:00:59.996
This module keeps that state true.

16
00:00:59.996 --> 00:01:03.021
A credential added by
hand, a new consent grant,

17
00:01:03.021 --> 00:01:05.409
an app nobody has used in ninety days,

18
00:01:05.409 --> 00:01:10.267
which is Microsoft Learn's threshold
for an unused app, or an owner who left:

19
00:01:10.267 --> 00:01:14.407
each one shows up as drift, against
a baseline someone approved.

20
00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:17.100
Then the evidence has to reach the ISSO in

21
00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:19.871
a form they can trust
without trusting you.

22
00:01:19.871 --> 00:01:24.770
Each run is packaged with a hash for
every file, and chained to the run before.

23
00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:28.983
The ISSO checks a package with
the standard checksum tool alone.

24
00:01:28.983 --> 00:01:33.820
And a period summary shows whether
the checks ran every day, with no gap.

25
00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:40.377
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

26
00:01:40.377 --> 00:01:43.120
which takes about seventy-five
minutes with the lab.

27
00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:45.811
You'll explain why the
drift run compares with a

28
00:01:45.811 --> 00:01:48.570
baseline only when a
reviewer approved its hash.

29
00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:50.305
You'll read the drift findings,

30
00:01:50.305 --> 00:01:53.845
and say when a credential counts
as added inside the pipeline.

31
00:01:53.845 --> 00:01:58.733
You'll compare two vintages of the A I
use-case inventory against the register.

32
00:01:58.733 --> 00:02:03.605
You'll package two runs, chained,
verify one with the checksum tool,

33
00:02:03.605 --> 00:02:06.132
and assemble a period for the ISSO.

34
00:02:06.132 --> 00:02:08.447
And you'll show that an edited file and a

35
00:02:08.447 --> 00:02:11.195
package chained out of
order are both refused.

36
00:02:11.195 --> 00:02:17.366
Before you start: Modules 1 and 2, because
the drift run runs their code unchanged;

37
00:02:17.366 --> 00:02:20.732
Module 4, for the credential
log; and Module 9.

38
00:02:20.732 --> 00:02:24.085
Lab 1 also needs Python
with pytest and PyYAML,

39
00:02:24.085 --> 00:02:27.090
and a shell with sha two fifty-six sum.

40
00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:36.270
Unit 1 is the guide's Step 6, run the
lifecycle, which has four parts: owner change,

41
00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:40.173
orphans, access reviews of
owners, and decommissioning,

42
00:02:40.173 --> 00:02:43.652
which disables first and
deletes only afterward.

43
00:02:43.652 --> 00:02:47.496
In the program's guidance, a
non-person identity's events come

44
00:02:47.496 --> 00:02:51.416
from its owning system and from
credential expiry, not from HR.

45
00:02:51.416 --> 00:02:55.943
Then the guide's Evidence section adds
the program's rule this module turns into

46
00:02:55.943 --> 00:02:59.235
code: run the checks on the
continuous monitoring cadence,

47
00:02:59.235 --> 00:03:03.077
and file their output with the date,
the tenant and the check version.

48
00:03:03.077 --> 00:03:09.177
The guide's validate step for Gate 6 names
NIST special publication eight hundred

49
00:03:09.177 --> 00:03:12.674
fifty-three controls A C
two, enhancement three;

50
00:03:12.674 --> 00:03:15.903
A C six, enhancement seven;
A U six; and C A seven.

51
00:03:15.903 --> 00:03:23.247
The FedRAMP K S I's it lines
up with are K S I I A M A A M,

52
00:03:23.247 --> 00:03:27.560
K S I M L A R V L and K S I M L A E V C.

53
00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:36.198
Unit 2 starts with what the evidence
has to show: state, events and drift.

54
00:03:36.198 --> 00:03:38.948
The run and the baseline
are the kit's design.

55
00:03:38.948 --> 00:03:40.811
One run writes one folder.

56
00:03:40.811 --> 00:03:45.583
It holds Chapter 1's inventory and
Chapter 2's ownership check, unchanged,

57
00:03:45.583 --> 00:03:47.730
plus this chapter's drift folder,

58
00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:52.185
and a run record that ties them
together by each task's manifest hash.

59
00:03:52.185 --> 00:03:55.027
A task that fails is recorded as an error,

60
00:03:55.027 --> 00:03:59.247
never as an empty result, so a
failed read can't look like a fix.

61
00:03:59.247 --> 00:04:04.429
The baseline, the program's design guidance,
is a fingerprint per identity: a hash

62
00:04:04.429 --> 00:04:09.923
over twenty governance columns, such as
owners, credentials, permissions and grants.

63
00:04:09.923 --> 00:04:12.661
Last logon and password age are left out,

64
00:04:12.661 --> 00:04:15.648
because they move every
day and aren't drift.

65
00:04:15.648 --> 00:04:18.769
And the baseline counts only
when it's passed with the

66
00:04:18.769 --> 00:04:21.748
hash a reviewer approved,
for this tenant and cloud.

67
00:04:21.748 --> 00:04:25.100
In a real run, a second person reviews it.

68
00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:31.950
Nine drift rules run against
the approved baseline.

69
00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:36.800
New and changed identities are warnings,
with the exact columns that changed.

70
00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:39.120
Ownerless identities are findings:

71
00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:43.450
orphan candidates in Chapter 2's
run, and this code disables nothing.

72
00:04:43.450 --> 00:04:46.102
An app with no sign-in
for over ninety days,

73
00:04:46.102 --> 00:04:49.689
or a credential that expires
within thirty, is a warning;

74
00:04:49.689 --> 00:04:53.667
both numbers are Microsoft
Learn's, from its recommendations

75
00:04:53.667 --> 00:04:57.800
to remove unused applications
and renew expiring credentials.

76
00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:02.325
An enabled directory account whose
password never expires is a finding.

77
00:05:02.325 --> 00:05:05.850
So is a credential added outside
the pipeline, one per key:

78
00:05:05.850 --> 00:05:09.825
Microsoft Learn's security
operations guide for applications

79
00:05:09.825 --> 00:05:13.275
rates a credential added to
an existing app as high risk.

80
00:05:13.275 --> 00:05:16.566
A new consent grant or app
permission is a warning,

81
00:05:16.566 --> 00:05:19.937
or a finding when it's
tenant-wide or high privilege.

82
00:05:19.937 --> 00:05:22.121
And the ninth is the honest one:

83
00:05:22.121 --> 00:05:25.788
an evidence gap is reported,
never treated as a pass.

84
00:05:25.788 --> 00:05:28.417
The key rule, the
program's design guidance:

85
00:05:28.417 --> 00:05:33.678
a credential counts as inside the pipeline
only when two independent records agree,

86
00:05:33.678 --> 00:05:38.630
the listed pipeline identity and the
rotation pipeline's credential log.

87
00:05:42.380 --> 00:05:45.480
Two more kinds of evidence
sit beside the drift.

88
00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:50.091
The review cycle reads the review
records that Chapters 7 and 9 build;

89
00:05:50.091 --> 00:05:54.055
this chapter doesn't run the
reviews, it reads their records.

90
00:05:54.055 --> 00:05:56.488
Following the program's design guidance,

91
00:05:56.488 --> 00:06:00.209
it flags an app holding Graph
permissions whose owners haven't

92
00:06:00.209 --> 00:06:03.930
reviewed it in ninety-two days,
and agent access with no end date.

93
00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:07.898
And it flags an agent with no
sponsor attestation in a year;

94
00:06:07.898 --> 00:06:13.105
Microsoft Learn's best practices for
Agent I D say every six to twelve months.

95
00:06:13.105 --> 00:06:16.586
And a kind of review record that
never arrived is a warning,

96
00:06:16.586 --> 00:06:19.342
because a missing record
isn't a passed review.

97
00:06:19.342 --> 00:06:23.483
Then once a year, the program's
vintage comparison checks this

98
00:06:23.483 --> 00:06:27.380
year's A I use-case inventory
against last year's, by use case.

99
00:06:27.380 --> 00:06:31.059
It catches a new piloted or
deployed system with no identity,

100
00:06:31.059 --> 00:06:36.061
a retired one whose identities are still
enabled, a changed authorization answer,

101
00:06:36.061 --> 00:06:40.610
and an entry that left the inventory
without being reported retired.

102
00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:47.523
Unit 3 is what the ISSO receives.

103
00:06:47.523 --> 00:06:49.833
Each run is packaged: the run itself,

104
00:06:49.833 --> 00:06:54.775
a summary with rule I D's and fingerprints
but no names, an evidence manifest,

105
00:06:54.775 --> 00:06:58.760
and a checksum file anyone can
verify with the standard tool.

106
00:06:58.760 --> 00:07:03.354
Before it writes anything, the
packager checks the run end to end:

107
00:07:03.354 --> 00:07:08.035
every manifest by its hash, nothing
extra, one tenant and one cloud.

108
00:07:08.035 --> 00:07:12.795
Each package records the previous
package's manifest hash, so the packages

109
00:07:12.795 --> 00:07:17.172
form a chain, and a replaced package
or a missing day shows up as a break.

110
00:07:17.172 --> 00:07:19.733
Then the period verifies every package,

111
00:07:19.733 --> 00:07:23.847
checks the chain and coverage,
and maps findings to each control.

112
00:07:23.847 --> 00:07:26.719
There's no signing, by
the kit's design: hashes,

113
00:07:26.719 --> 00:07:30.946
plus a locked time-based retention
policy on the evidence store,

114
00:07:30.946 --> 00:07:35.811
the write once, read many storage
Microsoft Learn documents for blob data.

115
00:07:35.811 --> 00:07:38.659
Each control reads
either evidence present,

116
00:07:38.659 --> 00:07:41.585
the assessor decides,
or evidence incomplete.

117
00:07:41.585 --> 00:07:45.190
The control mapping is the
program's design guidance.

118
00:07:48.940 --> 00:07:49.665
Try it.

119
00:07:49.665 --> 00:07:53.044
Steps one to six run in
the non-person identity

120
00:07:53.044 --> 00:07:55.890
folder of the examples, in PowerShell 7.

121
00:07:55.890 --> 00:08:00.074
Step one runs Microsoft's Sentinel
rule for new app credentials,

122
00:08:00.074 --> 00:08:05.127
the one Microsoft Learn's security
operations guide for applications links,

123
00:08:05.127 --> 00:08:08.048
over the fixture: eleven
tests pass, none fail,

124
00:08:08.048 --> 00:08:10.890
and forty of the file's
other tests aren't run.

125
00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:14.992
Step two lists what the run needs:
four Graph read permissions,

126
00:08:14.992 --> 00:08:18.853
Application, AuditLog, Directory
and User dot Read dot All.

127
00:08:18.853 --> 00:08:20.527
Nothing ends in ReadWrite.

128
00:08:20.527 --> 00:08:25.689
Step three runs the first day, the
twenty-ninth of October, with no baseline.

129
00:08:25.689 --> 00:08:30.716
Inventory, ownership and drift all report
OK, and the run becomes a baseline

130
00:08:30.716 --> 00:08:33.067
candidate, with fourteen findings,

131
00:08:33.067 --> 00:08:36.715
four warnings and five
information items, and its hash.

132
00:08:36.715 --> 00:08:40.990
Step four approves that baseline by
its hash and runs the second day.

133
00:08:40.990 --> 00:08:43.630
It compares with the first day's baseline,

134
00:08:43.630 --> 00:08:47.940
and the drift appears: eighteen
findings and eleven warnings.

135
00:08:51.690 --> 00:08:57.403
Step five reads day two's drift, grouped
by rule and severity: twelve rows.

136
00:08:57.403 --> 00:09:00.017
N P I D R F oh seven, three findings,

137
00:09:00.017 --> 00:09:04.741
holds the three credentials
added outside the pipeline.

138
00:09:04.741 --> 00:09:08.902
Notice what isn't there: the pipeline's
own two certificate rotations.

139
00:09:08.902 --> 00:09:11.958
Both were made by a listed
pipeline identity and

140
00:09:11.958 --> 00:09:15.090
are in the credential log,
so the two records agree.

141
00:09:15.090 --> 00:09:21.877
N P I D R F oh eight, one finding, is Mail
Automation's new grant for all users.

142
00:09:21.877 --> 00:09:26.163
You'll also see seven ownerless
identities, three enabled accounts

143
00:09:26.163 --> 00:09:30.066
whose passwords never expire,
and the review cycle's findings.

144
00:09:30.066 --> 00:09:33.790
Step six compares the A
I use-case inventory's

145
00:09:33.790 --> 00:09:37.615
2026 vintage with 2025's,
against the register.

146
00:09:37.615 --> 00:09:43.202
Five changes: one authorization changed,
one entry left, two new and one retired,

147
00:09:43.202 --> 00:09:47.690
for three findings, two warnings
and one information item.

148
00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:55.445
Step seven switches to a second
shell, a POSIX shell at the root of

149
00:09:55.445 --> 00:09:59.752
your copy of the repository, and
moves into the drift pipeline folder.

150
00:09:59.752 --> 00:10:04.278
The runs it packages are the repository's
copies of the two days you just made.

151
00:10:04.278 --> 00:10:08.752
Package day one, then day two,
chained to day one's manifest.

152
00:10:08.752 --> 00:10:10.990
Each prints one line of JSON.

153
00:10:10.990 --> 00:10:16.240
The second says nineteen findings are new,
eighty are open, and one is resolved.

154
00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:19.628
Step eight verifies the
second package three ways.

155
00:10:19.628 --> 00:10:23.115
The packager's own verify
reports twenty-one files.

156
00:10:23.115 --> 00:10:26.929
The standard checksum tool,
run against the checksum file,

157
00:10:26.929 --> 00:10:28.915
prints nothing and exits zero.

158
00:10:28.915 --> 00:10:33.254
And a search of the summary for the
fictional tenant's domain counts zero:

159
00:10:33.254 --> 00:10:35.640
no user principal name is in the summary.

160
00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:40.150
That's the check an ISSO can run
with nothing but standard tools.

161
00:10:43.900 --> 00:10:48.408
Step nine assembles the period for
the ISSO, from the twenty-eighth

162
00:10:48.408 --> 00:10:52.425
to the thirtieth of October, with
the A I inventory's vintage.

163
00:10:52.425 --> 00:10:53.575
It exits two.

164
00:10:53.575 --> 00:10:56.567
The chain has no breaks
and no coverage gaps,

165
00:10:56.567 --> 00:11:01.137
across two packages, with nineteen
findings opened and one closed.

166
00:11:01.137 --> 00:11:02.738
But complete is false.

167
00:11:02.738 --> 00:11:07.599
The summary's controls table shows why:
some chapters haven't sent their findings.

168
00:11:07.599 --> 00:11:12.087
A C two is evidence incomplete
without Chapter 3's findings.

169
00:11:12.087 --> 00:11:17.288
I A five waits on Chapters 4 and 6,
and I A nine on Chapters 5 and 8.

170
00:11:17.288 --> 00:11:21.513
The other controls read evidence
present, the assessor decides.

171
00:11:21.513 --> 00:11:25.788
That's honest: an incomplete period
says so, instead of passing.

172
00:11:25.788 --> 00:11:30.584
The summary also has a table for the
project plan's Gate 7, steady state,

173
00:11:30.584 --> 00:11:35.800
which the guide's G 6 covers, and a row
for each package with its manifest hash.

174
00:11:35.800 --> 00:11:38.530
The identity governance officer signs it.

175
00:11:42.280 --> 00:11:44.268
Step ten breaks it on purpose.

176
00:11:44.268 --> 00:11:47.931
Append one line to the packaged
summary, and verify again.

177
00:11:47.931 --> 00:11:51.468
It exits one: the summary
doesn't match its hash.

178
00:11:51.468 --> 00:11:55.006
Then try to package day one
as if it came after day two,

179
00:11:55.006 --> 00:11:56.981
chained to day two's manifest.

180
00:11:56.981 --> 00:12:01.392
It exits one too: this run isn't
later than the previous package.

181
00:12:01.392 --> 00:12:05.079
So an edited file and a swapped
package are both refused,

182
00:12:05.079 --> 00:12:09.405
and that's what lets the ISSO trust
the chain without trusting you.

183
00:12:09.405 --> 00:12:14.668
The step ends by linting the scheduled
workflow, with no findings and no warnings.

184
00:12:14.668 --> 00:12:19.856
Step eleven runs the tests in both folders:
five hundred thirteen Pester tests pass

185
00:12:19.856 --> 00:12:24.818
in PowerShell, and one hundred forty-two
pytest tests pass in the drift pipeline.

186
00:12:24.818 --> 00:12:27.870
Then delete the output
folder in both places.

187
00:12:31.620 --> 00:12:35.993
Lab 2 moves to your own test
tenant, worldwide or G C C,

188
00:12:35.993 --> 00:12:41.720
with Entra I D P one or P two, never
production, with fictional people.

189
00:12:41.720 --> 00:12:46.332
You also need Chapter 1's test
host and Chapter 2's Lab 2 inputs.

190
00:12:46.332 --> 00:12:50.531
Register a reader app with a certificate
and the four read permissions,

191
00:12:50.531 --> 00:12:52.595
run a baseline, and have it approved.

192
00:12:52.595 --> 00:12:57.257
Then make three changes by hand:
two secrets and one consent grant.

193
00:12:57.257 --> 00:13:01.096
Run the drift run, and check the
activity names it found against the

194
00:13:01.096 --> 00:13:04.795
admin center's audit log, because
that's what the fixture assumed.

195
00:13:04.795 --> 00:13:07.570
Then package and verify the test runs.

196
00:13:07.570 --> 00:13:11.670
To back it out, step seven removes
both secrets and the consent grant,

197
00:13:11.670 --> 00:13:14.598
the four permissions and
the Reader assignment,

198
00:13:14.598 --> 00:13:19.283
deletes the reader app and its certificate,
and deletes the runs and packages.

199
00:13:19.283 --> 00:13:21.790
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

200
00:13:25.540 --> 00:13:26.927
The federal requirement.

201
00:13:26.927 --> 00:13:31.449
These are NIST special publication
eight hundred fifty-three controls,

202
00:13:31.449 --> 00:13:34.915
and the module's table maps
each lab step to its evidence.

203
00:13:34.915 --> 00:13:37.877
For baseline configuration
and change control,

204
00:13:37.877 --> 00:13:40.690
the baseline candidate
and its approved hash.

205
00:13:40.690 --> 00:13:44.802
For continuous monitoring and account
management, the drift findings.

206
00:13:44.802 --> 00:13:48.980
For automated audit actions, audit
review and system monitoring,

207
00:13:48.980 --> 00:13:52.152
the events and the two
credential and grant rules.

208
00:13:52.152 --> 00:13:56.503
For disabling accounts, the sign-in
activity and the unused-app rule.

209
00:13:56.503 --> 00:13:59.477
For review of privileges,
the review cycle.

210
00:13:59.477 --> 00:14:03.259
And for the integrity of the
record, A U nine and S I seven,

211
00:14:03.259 --> 00:14:08.889
the evidence manifest, the checksum file
and the summary, and the period manifest.

212
00:14:08.889 --> 00:14:13.528
K S I's are FedRAMP twenty X indicators
for cloud service providers.

213
00:14:13.528 --> 00:14:17.968
The agency lines its evidence up
with what its providers report;

214
00:14:17.968 --> 00:14:19.978
it doesn't report K S I's itself.

215
00:14:19.978 --> 00:14:23.580
Which file supports which
indicator is the program's mapping.

216
00:14:27.330 --> 00:14:29.305
Check yourself with two questions.

217
00:14:29.305 --> 00:14:33.093
First: a certificate rotation
appears in the audit log.

218
00:14:33.093 --> 00:14:35.367
When does it count as inside the pipeline?

219
00:14:35.367 --> 00:14:39.665
The answer is b: when the initiator
is a listed pipeline identity,

220
00:14:39.665 --> 00:14:44.368
and the target and key I D are in the
rotation pipeline's credential log.

221
00:14:44.368 --> 00:14:49.777
That's the program's design guidance:
two independent records must agree, so a

222
00:14:49.777 --> 00:14:53.041
stolen pipeline credential
used outside the

223
00:14:53.041 --> 00:14:55.617
log still shows up as
N P I D R F oh seven.

224
00:14:55.617 --> 00:15:00.104
Second: you append a line to a
packaged summary and run verify.

225
00:15:00.104 --> 00:15:01.117
What happens?

226
00:15:01.117 --> 00:15:02.342
The answer is b.

227
00:15:02.342 --> 00:15:06.154
It exits one, because the
summary doesn't match its hash.

228
00:15:06.154 --> 00:15:07.893
That's what you did in step ten.

229
00:15:07.893 --> 00:15:10.319
The module page has three more questions,

230
00:15:10.319 --> 00:15:13.180
each linked to the
section that answers it.

231
00:15:16.930 --> 00:15:17.755
To sum up.

232
00:15:17.755 --> 00:15:21.398
You ran two days of drift
against an approved baseline,

233
00:15:21.398 --> 00:15:24.960
found three credentials
added outside the pipeline,

234
00:15:24.960 --> 00:15:30.223
compared two A I inventory vintages,
packaged and chained both days, assembled

235
00:15:30.223 --> 00:15:35.080
a period for the ISSO, and saw an edited
file and a swapped package refused.

236
00:15:35.080 --> 00:15:39.052
The evidence an ISSO keeps for
Gate 6 is each day's package,

237
00:15:39.052 --> 00:15:43.362
with its evidence manifest,
checksum file, summary and the run;

238
00:15:43.362 --> 00:15:47.081
the approved baseline's
hash; the vintage comparison;

239
00:15:47.081 --> 00:15:52.743
and the period's manifest and summary,
signed by the identity governance officer.

240
00:15:52.743 --> 00:15:55.817
Before you ship, work through
the chapter's checklist.

241
00:15:55.817 --> 00:16:00.768
Next is Module 11, the last:
deploy to test, then production.

242
00:16:00.768 --> 00:16:04.638
It runs every gate's code in one
safe order, with a preflight,

243
00:16:04.638 --> 00:16:09.610
a smoke test, an evidence record per
requirement and a rehearsed rollback.
